WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:38.199
Believe it or not, there’s still land on
earth that’s not part of any country.

00:00:38.199 --> 00:00:40.760
Bir Tawil is that land.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:43.949
This 800 square mile (2100 square kilometer)
area between Egypt and Sudan is claimed by

00:00:43.949 --> 00:00:45.710
neither Egypt nor Sudan.

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:50.820
To understand why this is, we have to go back,
to 1899.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.890
At the turn of the 20th century, Britain had
a large and rapidly expanding empire across

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:55.890
the world.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:59.670
A large part of this empire was in Africa,
where colonialism had taken root.

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:04.080
Britain held a complex level of authority
in North-East Africa, and through that got

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.890
to decide that the border between Sudan and
Egypt was going to be the 22nd parallel.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:12.500
The problem with this border was that it made
no consideration to the placement of actual

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:18.090
people, it just put a big line in the sand
and arbitrarily split up tribes and people.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:23.360
In 1902, Britain decided to draw a new administrative
boundary that put into consideration that

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.930
use of the land by various tribes.

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:30.229
That way, tribes wouldn’t be living in one
territory and grazing their cattle in another.

00:01:30.229 --> 00:01:35.369
Fast forward 114 years, and both territories
are now fully fledged countries—no longer

00:01:35.369 --> 00:01:37.170
under British rule.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:42.700
The problem is, Sudan now recognizes the 1902
administrative boundary as the real border,

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:49.090
while Egypt recognized the 1899 border on
the 22nd parallel as the real border.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:53.049
What this means is that Bir Tawil is South
of Egypt’s border—so Egypt thinks it’s

00:01:53.049 --> 00:01:58.970
part of Sudan—while also north of Sudan’s
border—so Sudan thinks it’s part of Egypt.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:02.590
This doesn’t really create many problems
though, because there’s a whopping zero

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:04.649
people living in Bir Tawil.

00:02:04.649 --> 00:02:11.010
There’s not even any roads or buildings—it’s
just 800 square miles of nothing…ruled by

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:13.830
nobody.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:17.750
This same border issue does create issues
in other places though.

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:22.010
The Hala’ib triangle is 10 times bigger
that Bir Tawil, and, instead of being claimed

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.780
by no country, it’s claimed by two countries.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:29.610
That’s because its North of what Egypt thinks
is the border while simultaneously south of

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:32.170
what Sudan thinks is the border.

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:36.320
The two countries actually care about this
piece of land because there are things.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:37.970
There’s a road!

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:39.470
And sea access!

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:41.600
And 1,000 residents!

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.780
Currently, the land is administered by Egypt,
but let’s get back to Bir Tawil.

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:52.110
In his 2014 book, Alastair Bonnett said that
Bir Tawil is “…the only place on the planet

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:55.360
that is both habitable and unclaimed.”

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:00.200
The biggest unclaimed piece of land is the
Marie Byrd land, but, that’s not considered

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:04.700
to be habitable land, since, you know, it’s
in Antartica.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:09.570
There’s one other area of technically unclaimed
land on the Danube River between Croatia and

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:15.120
Serbia due to a similar border dispute, however,
practically, the land, while on a map not

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.030
part of either country, is well-divided administratively.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:23.951
So, Bir Tawil is the only place on earth where
people might actually be able to create a

00:03:23.951 --> 00:03:25.660
new country.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:27.060
So what’s stopping people?

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:28.950
Well, not much.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:32.650
Scores of people have claimed the land as
their own country, but if my video on How

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:36.560
to Create a Country taught you anything, it’s
that it doesn’t matter if you think you’re

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.290
a country, it matters if other people think
you’re a country.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:44.930
No recognized country currently recognizes
any claim to Bir Tawil likely because nobody

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:49.230
has set up a government or permanent population
in Bir Tawil.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:55.260
While the land is habitable, there is no infrastructure
to bring in supplies to support a settlement.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:59.350
With it being a desert and all, agriculture
doesn’t do so well, so any prospective country

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:03.310
founder would need to build a road or airport
to bring in food.

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:07.960
In addition, there really aren’t any natural
resources, so it’s unlikely that a country

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:11.280
established in the area would be self-sustaining
economically.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:17.930
However, if someone was able to set up a democratic
government in the area with a permanent population,

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:22.090
its entirely possible that they would gain
recognition, and there would be a 197th country

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:31.120
in the world—or 194th… or 193rd.. or,
it really just depends on who you ask.

